  "system": "You are rating how a set of arm movements appear in a single-channel surface EMG\nrecording of the triceps brachii (long head).\n\nFor each movement you will assign one ordinal level per feature, and you will also\nplace all 12 movements in rank order on each feature.\n\nLEVELS -- the same three for every feature, defined by position within this set:\nhigh   = among the top 4 of the 12 movements on this feature\nmedium = in the middle 4\nlow    = among the bottom 4\n\nFEATURES (each definition states what a HIGH value means):\n\nT1 - Rate of EMG rise (RER)\n  Peak of the derivative of the rectified, low-pass envelope -- how steeply muscle activity climbs as the movement starts.\n  Direction: high = climbs faster at onset.\n\nT2 - Offset rate\n  Rate of envelope decay at deactivation -- how steeply muscle activity falls away once the movement ends.\n  Direction: high = falls away faster at the end.\n\nT3 - Burst duration\n  Onset-to-offset interval divided by movement duration -- what fraction of the movement the muscle is active for.\n  Direction: high = active for a larger fraction of the movement.\n\nA1 - Peak amplitude\n  Peak of the envelope as a percentage of the session maximum voluntary contraction -- how hard the muscle is driven.\n  Direction: high = a larger peak.\n\nS1 - Median frequency (MDF)\n  The frequency dividing the power spectrum into equal-power halves, measured on the raw band-passed signal.\n  Direction: high = a higher median frequency.\n\nHOW TO ANSWER\n- Judge each movement relative to the other 11 in this set, not\n  against movement in general.\n- Use the full range. The set is constructed to span each feature.\n- Rank each feature from the highest value to the lowest, listing every movement\n  exactly once.\n- Return only JSON matching the supplied schema. No prose, no explanation.",
  "user": "MOVEMENTS\nM01: The body is positioned in a way such that the upper body is parallel to the ground and supported by a free arm. The other arm holds a weight and is tucked to the side of the body such that the upper arm is parallel to he body and the elbow is bent 90 degrees. The person then extends the elbow very slowly, coming to a stop at the full extention, then bringing the arm back down to the starting position.\nM02: The dancer begins with a fully bent elbow with their hand touching the chest with the index finger extended. The arm is extended forward with as much speed as possible to near full extention, and stopping all movement for a frozen effect.\nM03: The body begins in an upright position, holding a weight with both hands behind the head. The elbows are bent, and pointing straight up, near the ears. The person then extends both arms to move the weight upwards to full extention very slowly, briefly coming to a stop and coming back down.\nM04: The triceps are activated very slowly, and without pause loosened very slowly.\nM05: The dancer begins with a fully bent elbow with their hand touching the chest with the index finger extended. The arm is extended forward in a controllable speed to near full extention, and stopping all movement for a frozen effect.\nM06: The body is positioned in a way such that the upper body is parallel to the ground and supported by a free arm. The other arm holds a weight and is tucked to the side of the body such that the upper arm is parallel to he body and the elbow is bent 90 degrees. The person then extends the elbow with as much speed as they are able to, coming to a stop at the full extention, then bringing the arm back down to the starting position.\nM07: The triceps are activated with as much speed as possible, and without pause loosened with as much speed as possible.\nM08: The dancer begins with a fully bent elbow with their hand touching the chest with the index finger extended. The arm is extended forward very slowly to near full extention, and stopping all movement for a frozen effect.\nM09: The body begins in an upright position, holding a weight with both hands behind the head. The elbows are bent, and pointing straight up, near the ears. The person then extends both arms to move the weight upwards to full extention with as much speed as they can, briefly coming to a stop and coming back down.\nM10: The triceps are activated fashion, and without pause loosened.\nM11: The body begins in an upright position, holding a weight with both hands behind the head. The elbows are bent, and pointing straight up, near the ears. The person then extends both arms to move the weight upwards to full extention in a speed which allows for the best control, briefly coming to a stop and coming back down.\nM12: The body is positioned in a way such that the upper body is parallel to the ground and supported by a free arm. The other arm holds a weight and is tucked to the side of the body such that the upper arm is parallel to he body and the elbow is bent 90 degrees. The person then extends the elbow in a speed that allows for best control in the movement, coming to a stop at the full extention, then bringing the arm back down to the starting position.\n\nAssign every movement a level on all 5 features, and give a full 12-item ranking per feature. Refer to movements by their identifier only.",
